    "case_summary": "A 53-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B virus infection (>30 years) presented with weakness, bloating, anorexia, and decreased urine output for 10 days. He denied any history of infectious disease, such as typhoid or tuberculosis, chronic disease, such as hypertension or diabetes, or recent surgery or trauma.\n\nBlood pressure on admission was 153/89 mmHg. On July 15, patient developed fever (39°C) with obvious tenderness of the left lumbar vertebra. By July 16, temperature increased to 40.2°C, accompanied by chills, severe low back pain, and forced position.\n\n- Laboratory tests (on admission):    - ALT: 160 U/L (ref: 0-40)    - AST: 167 U/L (ref: 0-40)    - Serum albumin: 28 g/L (ref: 40-60)    - Total bilirubin: 77.4 μmol/L (ref: 5.1-19)    - Direct bilirubin: 22.4 μmol/L (ref: 0-6)    - WBC: 3.51 × 109/L (ref: 3.5-8.5)    - Neutrophils: 58.1% (ref: 55%-70%)    - Lymphocytes: 22.25% (ref: 20%-40%)    - Hemoglobin: 112 g/L (ref: 120-165)    - Platelets: 74 × 109/L (ref: 125-350)    - Ascites cell count: 200/μL  - Imaging studies:    - Abdominal ultrasound showed liver cirrhosis and ascites with maximum anteroposterior diameter of 132 mm      - Microbiology:    - Blood culture positive with grayish white colonies on blood agar and green colonies on TCBS agar    - 16S rRNA sequencing confirmed organism identity    - Antibiotic susceptibility testing showed sensitivity to multiple antibiotics",
